Describe Fomantic UI dropdowns to assistive technologies.

Fomantic 2.9.4 ships exactly one ARIA attribute in its whole dropdown
component -- an aria-labelledby on the search input -- and the 2.10 branch
ships the same one. A dropdown built from a <select> hides that select with
display: none, so the <label for="..."> of the form names a control which is
no longer in the accessibility tree, and what is left for everybody else is a
nameless, roleless div[tabindex="0"].

A dropdown standing for a form control becomes a combobox over a listbox of
options, named from the label pointing at the select, at the container, or at
the name of the hidden input the value is kept in. Options carry their
selected state, and the control points at the entry the cursor sits on.

What the keyboard was missing comes with it: enter and space open what is
closed, escape closes and keeps the focus, home and end reach both ends of a
list the way the arrows walk it, and the focus is caught back when the
component drops it on the document body.

Dropdowns revealing a menu are left alone: their menu is a child of the very
element they are opened from, so describing them needs a change of markup
rather than of attributes.

Applied to semantic/src at build time by the fomantic-a11y gulp task.
Reported upstream as https://github.com/fomantic/Fomantic-UI/issues/1332
